 The parity harness and the campaign table exporter read these files, not the live ones at the repo root, so stream B can change the JS rules during the sprint without breaking the campaign port's golden test.
 
 **Catching up:** when stream B changes a campaign rule, port the change to `godot/campaign/`, re-copy the six files from the new commit, run `node godot/campaign/test/node/export_campaign.js` to refresh `godot/campaign/data/campaign_tables.json`, run the parity test, and update the commit numbers above.
+
+## Proposed module-fit exception (Codex, Oct 31, 2026)
+
+The pinned oracle above remains the baseline. This candidate changes only module compatibility in core.js and its fleet-screen/action consumers in campaign_run.js, matching the browser/Godot candidate. It does not re-pin the rest of either file or adopt unrelated gameplay candidates.
+
+## Proposed Saro lineage exception (Codex, Oct 31, 2026)
+
+The original pin above is preserved. This candidate adds the opt-in Saro lineage module/snapshot and mastery getter to core.js, and the corresponding guarded primary-barrage resolver to battle.js. The campaign oracle also carries the matching guarded reward/getter and browser picker changes in campaign_meta.js. The Godot campaign candidate carries the native reward adapter. All other oracle content remains at its original pin. This is a scoped prototype, not a whole-oracle re-pin or a whole-port clearance.
